The Walt Disney Co. smashed Wall Street expectations in its latest quarter, its fiscal Q4, with all three of its core divisions: Entertainment, experiences and sports, posting double digit year over year growth despite what the company described as a &#8220;challenging macroeconomic environment.&#8221;

Revenues rose 16% to $25.9 billion in the quarter with segment operating income soaring by 43% to $5 billion, and adjusted earnings per share rising to $1.77. The company told the street that it expects to continue seeing double digit EPS growth in fiscal 2027. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories TV Brenda Song Says She Felt "Underpaid and Overworked" on Disney's 'The Suite Life' Shows TV 'Frozen' Is Getting the 'A Real Bug's Life' Treatment With Nat Geo, Disney+ and Hulu's 'The Real Frozen'

In the quarterly shareholder letter, CEO Josh D'Amaro and CEO Hugh Johnston once again outlined the moves the company is making around their three core pillars of investing in IP and creativity; Using technology to power storytelling and increase returns; and the One Disney model that they are implementing.

&#8220;Creativity and the pursuit of creative excellence will always be at the core of our company,&#8221; they wrote. &#8220;The increased engagement we saw across our streaming platforms and networks in the fourth quarter, which directly supports our revenue growth, reflects the strength of our creative talent, our focused investment in storytelling, and our leading sports rights portfolio.&#8221;

Disney's entertainment division saw revenues of $11.9 billion and operating income of $1.3 billion, thanks to continues growth in streaming at Disney+ and Hulu, and due to the theatrical performance of Spider Man: Brand New Day .

Experiences had revenues of $10.1 billion and operating income of $2.25 billion as per capita spending, increased domestic parks attendance and growth internationally helped offset a decrease in international visitors. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Sports had revenue of $4.6 billion and operating income of $1.4 billion, with the company touting strong football viewership and ESPN's contributions to the overall streaming ecosystem.

But the company also disclosed a plan to change how it reports earnings to Wall Street, and while some of the changes are logical (consumer products will now be part of the entertainment division, A+E was sold to Hearst), the company will also end standalone disclosure of streaming operating income, which, combined with the move to pare back subscriber numbers, will mean less granular data around the sector.

&#8220;We have made strong progress scaling our SVOD businesses and, as a result, see a long runway for growth and margin expansion at Disney Entertainment,&#8221; the executives write in the letter. &#8220;We no longer believe the supplemental disclosure of SVOD operating income reflects this segment focus and strategy. Accordingly, we do not plan to continue providing this disclosure going forward.&#8221;

SVOD revenue was $6.1 billion in the quarter, with operating income of $824 million.

The letter also addressed the layoffs that have flowed through the company over the course of the year, with the executives writing that the company will &#8220;realize annual run-rate operating expense savings of approximately $3 billion by fiscal 2029. While these efficiencies will be used primarily to fund future growth, we do expect a portion to drop to the bottom line over time.&#8221;

In fiscal 2027, Disney projects double digit EPS growth, segment operating income growth of about 8%, and capex of $9 billion thanks to its long-term experiences investment.

The BBC and Canada's CBC have confirmed their adaptation of E.M. Forster's A Passage to India , casting Ali Fazal , Fiona Shaw and Tom Mison as leads.

The beloved book just turned 100. Set against the backdrop of the British Raj and directed by Emmy-winner Richie Mehta ( Delhi Crime) , the series will chart Aziz's optimistic attempts to prove to his Indian friends that cross-cultural friendship is possible under British rule, inspiring people to try and forge meaningful connections.

Aziz is being played by Fazal, an Indian star who has appeared in the likes of Victoria & Abdul and Mirzapur . Killing Eve star is Mrs. Moore, visiting her son Ronny (Gwilym Lee) in India, while Mison ( See) is Fielding, an elusive educator who grows close to Aziz. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News 'Doctor Who': BBC Draws Up Producer Shortlist As Race To Regenerate Iconic Series Moves To Next Stage News BBC Boss Confronted By Angry Staff Over "Banning" Of Trans Rights Symbols In TV Shows

The series is one of the most high-profile BBC adaptations in a while and counts Canada's CBC as co-producer. Nisha Parti's Parti Productions is making A Passage to India with Working Title and Mehta's indie A Passage Post. Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

Other cast includes Naseeruddin Shah ( Paar, The League of Extraordinary Gentlemen ) as Godbole, a fellow educator at Fielding's college and spiritual anchor in the face of the events unfolding around him. Shahana Goswami ( Santosh, Four Years Later ) is Amrit Rao, Aziz's elegant, intellectual and sophisticated barrister, who has recently qualified as India's first woman lawyer. Danish Hussain ( Haq, Tanaav ) is Hamidullah, Aziz's uncle and best friend.

The show was first revealed by Working Title exec Surian Fletcher-Jones who said at the time it would &#8220;reclaim colonial history" and &#8220;turn the novel on its head.&#8221;

Mehta said: &#8220;Adapting A Passage to India has been a dream of mine for as long as I've been telling stories. To reinterpret the complex and intriguing characters that E.M. Forster masterfully conjured from his experiences over a century ago, and finding in them a relevance that resonates so deeply with today's world, has been a real joy.&#8221;

Parti added: &#8220;After almost ten years of work alongside Richie, it's an honour to be bringing this epic story to television for the first time. To tell the story of British colonial rule, which shaped all our families, from an Indian perspective feels like a first. This beautiful novel tackles that and so much more. We've seen many romanticised versions of India on screen over the years, so I'm so grateful to Working Title Television and Universal Global Television for understanding why our version needs to be told now and helping bring it to life, and to the BBC and CBC for being such brilliant partners.&#8221;

Ali Fazal (&#8220;Mirzapur&#8221;), Tom Mison and Fiona Shaw are leading the cast of &#8220; A Passage to India ,&#8221; Emmy-winning &#8220;Delhi Crime&#8221; filmmaker Richie Mehta's five-part series version of E.M. Forster's classic novel for the BBC and the Canadian Broadcasting Corporation.

Mehta wrote the adaptation and is now directing it on location in India.

Set in the 1920s, the drama follows Aziz (Fazal), a local doctor who has spent years caring for others and who sets out to convince his Indian friends that real friendship with the British can survive colonial rule. His hopes start to come apart after an unexplained episode at the Marabar caves, which leaves him accused of assault. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories Taiwan’s Chunghwa Telecom Unveils 10 Content Investments, From 'Gunshot' to 'Call of Lobster' ‘Pokemon’ Anime Studio OLM Trains AI Coloring Tool on Just Three Frames in Japan Government-Backed Project

The scandal stirs up violent feeling and builds to a trial that grips the public. As the case plays out, the lives of everyone involved are upended, and Aziz and those closest to him begin to grasp how deeply colonization has shaped them.

Mison (&#8220;De Gaulle: Resistance,&#8221; &#8220;See&#8221;) plays Fielding, an elusive teacher who becomes close to Aziz. BAFTA winner Shaw (&#8220;Bad Sisters,&#8221; &#8220;Killing Eve&#8221;) is Mrs. Moore, a woman who has led a varied life and who comes to India to see her son Ronny (Gwilym Lee, &#8220;The Great,&#8221; &#8220;Bohemian Rhapsody&#8221;) and set up a match between him and Adela (Clare Hughes, &#8220;Ponies,&#8221; &#8220;Ten Pound Poms&#8221;).

Naseeruddin Shah (&#8220;Paar,&#8221; &#8220;The League of Extraordinary Gentlemen&#8221;) plays Godbole, who teaches alongside Fielding at his college and offers spiritual grounding as the crisis deepens. Shahana Goswami (&#8220;Santosh,&#8221; &#8220;Four Years Later&#8221;) is Amrit Rao, Aziz's barrister, who has just become India's first woman lawyer. Danish Hussain (&#8220;Haq,&#8221; &#8220;Tanaav&#8221;) plays Hamidullah, Aziz's uncle and closest friend, who doubts the cross-cultural bonds Aziz believes in. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366],[300,450]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

&#8220;Adapting &#8216;A Passage to India' has been a dream of mine for as long as I've been telling stories,&#8221; Mehta said. &#8220;To reinterpret the complex and intriguing characters that E.M. Forster masterfully conjured from his experiences over a century ago, and finding in them a relevance that resonates so deeply with today's world, has been a real joy, and I believe it makes for incredibly gripping drama.&#8221;

Parti Productions (&#8220;The Boy with the Topknot&#8221;), Working Title Television (&#8220;Jo Nesb&#xF8;'s Detective Hole&#8221;) and Mehta's A Passage Post, a subsidiary of his Poor Man's Productions (&#8220;Poacher&#8221;), are producing the show. The BBC and CBC commissioned it for BBC One, BBC iPlayer, CBC TV and CBC Gem, and it qualifies as an official U.K.-Canada co-production. Working Title Television sits within Universal Studio Group's Universal Global Television division. NBCUniversal Global TV Distribution is handling worldwide sales.

Nisha Parti, founder of Parti Productions and an executive producer on the show, said she was honored to be bringing the story to television after almost 10 years of working with Mehta. &#8220;To tell the story of British colonial rule, which shaped all our families, from an Indian perspective feels like a first. This beautiful novel tackles that and so much more,&#8221; she said. &#8220;We've seen many romanticized versions of India on screen over the years, so I'm so grateful to Working Title Television and Universal Global Television for understanding why our version needs to be told now and helping bring it to life, and to the BBC and CBC for being such brilliant partners.&#8221;

Mehta and Parti serve as executive producers along with Working Title Television's Surian Fletcher-Jones, Eric Fellner and Tim Bevan, the BBC's Nawfal Faizullah, CBC's Nicola Makoway and Roma Khanna. Kristian Dench is series producer.

&#8220;&#8216;A Passage to India' is one of the great stories about friendship, and how quickly suspicion and power can tear it apart,&#8221; said Fletcher-Jones, who is head of drama at Working Title Television. &#8220;Richie has understood that from the very start, and his scripts handle it with real care and honesty.&#8221;

Lindsay Salt, director of BBC Drama, said Mehta's pitch had blown the broadcaster away. &#8220;He has crafted a version of extraordinary depth and sensitivity, and we're thrilled to be working with him and our partners at Parti Productions, Working Title Television, A Passage Post and CBC,&#8221; she said. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-variety-article-mid-articleX-uid2' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["btf","mid-articleX","mid"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-articleX") .addSize([[320,480],[300,250],[300,251],[620,366]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Forster's 1924 novel has been adapted before. Santha Rama Rau turned it into a stage play in 1960, and Waris Hussein directed a version of that play for BBC One's &#8220;Play of the Month&#8221; strand in 1965. David Lean's 1984 feature, also drawn from Rau's play, earned 11 Oscar nominations and won two, including best supporting actress for Peggy Ashcroft as Mrs. Moore.

CBC plans to bring the series to viewers in Canada, the U.K. and beyond, working with the BBC and NBCUniversal. Each of the five episodes runs 60 minutes.

The CEO of Channel 4 has said &#8220;we cannot apologize enough&#8221; to a woman who twice made complaints about Russell Brand and did not feel they were dealt with properly.

Priya Dogra said it is &#8220;almost unforgivable&#8221; that the woman, who went by the name Bella in The Times &#8216; investigation into Brand, said she complained to Channel 4 in both 2009 and 2023.

Dogra was speaking in front of the influential Digital, Culture, Media & Sport committee, whose chair Caroline Dinenage had recently spoken to Bella. Brand, now serving more than 10 years in prison, was convicted of sexually assaulting Bella in 2009 by placing his head in her groin and demanding a massage. In a victim impact statement, read out in court last week, Bella accused Channel 4 of brushing the sexual assault under the carpet . Dogra's predecessor Alex Mahon previously apologized to Bella. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News Russell Brand's "Appalling" Prison Message Being "Urgently" Investigated, Says 10 Downing Street News HBO Max Buys British Flatshare Comedy 'Break Clause' Starring Samuel Bottomley, Lara Ricote & 'Rooster's Charly Clive

Today, Dogra said Channel 4 will not open a new inquiry but apologized again to Bella. Since the 2023 complaint, she said Channel 4 has expanded its anonymous Speak Up whistleblowing service and reports regularly to the board about &#8220;incidents that have come up on all productions.&#8221; Production companies working with Channel 4 will now have to be mandatorily registered with CIISA, the new industry whistleblowing service that launched a few weeks back, according to Dogra. Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); }

Dogra was questioned on why a TV producer, who was accused in court of facilitating the massage then laughing afterwards, went on to work for Channel 4 long after 2009. Dogra said that when Channel 4 previously probed the Brand accusations it examined 31,000 documents, but even the findings about Brand at that stage were &#8220;rumor and speculation.&#8221; She referenced that this producer has stepped back from his production company since Brand was found guilty. She did not name him, but she is referring to Jack Bayles, Various Artists' head of comedy.

&#8220;This is not what we want to be known for&#8221;

In the wake of Brand and David Walliams, Dogra sat down with industry leaders and UK Culture Secretary Lisa Nandy yesterday to discuss next steps in ridding the entertainment industry of abuse, harassment and bad behavior. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Speaking about the entertainment industry more broadly, Dogra said she &#8220;wants to express my dismay and frustration that we end up in these cycles.&#8221;

&#8220;This is not what we want to be known for,&#8221; she added. &#8220;We want to be known for a positive impact on society and the economy but not these stories dominating the airwaves.&#8221;

She said her decision to axe Married at First Sight UK after rape allegations proves she is willing to act to avoid &#8220;commercial interests overriding our principles.&#8221; &#8220;We will walk away from a big show or a big personality that doesn't align with our values,&#8221; she added. &#8220;I've had to do that on [ MAFS UK] and others will have to make similar decisions.&#8221;

It's been a whirlwind 48 hours for Robert Pattinson .

The Hollywood Reporter heard from sources that the British star only found out he'd been tapped to succeed Daniel Craig as James Bond on Sunday morning, when Denis Villeneuve &#x2014;&#xA0;who's helming the next Bond movie &#x2014;&#xA0;called him. Producer Amy Pascal was said to inform the other candidates.

Within a day, the whole world knew too. Amazon MGM Studios, which bought the creative rights to the Bond franchise from producer siblings Michael G. Wilson and Barbara Broccoli just last year, released the news via the character's official social media accounts. Just before 7 a.m. PT on Monday, a tuxedo-wearing Pattinson emerged from the shadows, gun in hand. His announcement post has accrued over 1.2 million likes on X at the time of writing. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-article-mid-article-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid-articleX","mid","mid-article"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[300,250],[2,2],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4],[300,251]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Related Stories News Over 400 U.K. Industry Figures Demand End to "Culture of Open Secrets" After Russell Brand Trial Movies Tallinn Market Debates Estonia's Film Sector and How Foreign Shoots "Bring Both Value and Tension"

Thanks to Robert Eggers , who directed Pattinson in his 2019 period horror-fantasy The Lighthouse , we now know what the next Bond was up to when that news became public and sent the internet into a tailspin. During an on-camera interview with IGN , Eggers was asked if, while Pattinson's character killed a seagull during the making of The Lighthouse , he ever stopped to consider: &#8220;There goes the next James Bond.&#8221;

&#8220;No, but I'm not surprised that it panned out the way that it did,&#8221; replied the Nosferatu filmmaker. &#8220;And also, crazily enough, he was watching a screening of Werwulf when the news broke, and I was just texting with him about it this morning,&#8221; he said, laughing.

Werwulf , set for release on Dec. 25 in the U.S., stars Eggers regulars Aaron Taylor-Johnson, Lily-Rose Depp, Willem Dafoe and Ralph Ineson, and is set to chronicle a cursed farmer's transformation into a monstrous creature in 13th-century England. Coincidentally, Taylor-Johnson was among the rumored Bond contenders for a long time. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid","mid-article2","mid-articleX"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[320,480],[620,366],[300,450],[4,4]]) .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

So what did our new Bond think of Werwulf ? &#8220;Oh, he loved it, of course,&#8221; added Eggers.

Pattinson, who this year alone has starred in The Drama , The Odyssey , Primetime and still has Dune 3 to come, said on Monday through an Amazon MGM Studios press release: &#8220;James Bond is one of the most legendary characters in cinema, and to be entrusted with 007 is the honor of a lifetime. I'm deeply grateful to Denis, Amy, David, Tanya and everyone at Amazon MGM Studios for their belief in me. To say this role is a dream come true is an understatement. I couldn't be more excited about this partnership and the work ahead with this fantastic team.&#8221;

He won a months-long race for the role, beating fellow screen-testers Callum Turner, Josh O'Connor and Jack Lowden. Villeneuve, who recently worked with Pattinson on the final Dune outing, is directing a script from Peaky Blinders mastermind Steven Knight.

The 40-year-old actor is also already the face of DC Studio's Batman . THR took a deep dive into why his casting is t he smart but safe choice as the Bond franchise enters a new era. Fan reaction to the announcement is here &#x2014; and some are suggesting Pattinson's starring role in Twilight predicted his future Bond role .

In fact, Pattinson might have even manifested it . In 2012, during the height of his Twilight fame, the actor was asked in an interview if he could be playing 007 in 10 years' time. &#8220;Yeah, definitely,&#8221; he replied, &#8220;but more like 20 years. [&#8230;] I think there'd be nothing worse than, like, &#x2018;Let's get a fresh-faced Bond!' That would be the worst idea in the world. After Daniel Craig, you have to have some baggage. It would be ridiculous to reinvent [him] as some young posh kid. Or, if [he] was, I'd have to get so tortured in the first few scenes. I'd have to do the first film with one arm or something.&#8221;

Deadline has partnered with The Brit List to profile some of the emerging writers who have made this year's ranking of the best unproduced UK film and TV projects. Launched in 2007, The Brit List has previously featured projects including The King's Speech and Responsible Child . Here, we profile Gemma Addy , who has topped the list with her movie script Dig For Victory .

Gemma Addy harked back to her grandmothers' experiences during World War Two when she set about writing Dig for Victory, which has topped this year's Brit List.

Addy was in the midst of working on a different project about female bootleggers in the 1920s when she came across a photograph of a girl working on a vegetable patch during the war, which captivated her. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dsk-tab-mid-article1-uid0' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article1","mid","mid-articleX","mid-article","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article") .addSize([[2,2],[300,250],[620,350],[300,251],[501,282],[3,3],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; }); Watch on Deadline if ( !window.pmc.harmony?.isEventAdScheduledTime() ) { pmcCnx.cmd.push(function() { pmcCnx({ settings: { plugins: { pmcAtlasMG: { iabPlcmt: 2, } } }, playerId: '32fe25c4-79aa-406a-af44-69b41e969e71', playlistId: '0199e506-e642-7728-8264-463efe71337b', }).render("connatix_contextual_player_div"); }); } else { // This should only be get called when page cache is not cleared and it's event time. window.pmc.harmony?.switchToHarmonyPlayer(); } Related Stories News Brit List 2026: Crime Series & Coming-Of-Age Movie Lead List Of Best Unproduced Screenplays News Writer-Performer Jack Holden Channels 'It's A Sin' For Raucous Gay Liberation Movement TV Drama 'Revolting' - Brit List 2025

&#8220;She'd been growing things for the war effort and was holding the most enormous bunch of carrots you've ever seen,&#8221; said Addy. &#8220;They were bigger than she was, and she was smiling at the camera. For whatever reason, when I looked at the photo, I thought, &#8216;Wow, this is probably the best time of your life.' She got to be outside growing vegetables, with all the freedom, independence and recognition that came with it.&#8221;

Addy's attention was grabbed by the picture and she recalled the story of both her grandmothers, one of whom looked after evacuees during the war and the other was a Red Cross nurse in London. &#8220;They always spoke about the war, strange as it sounds, as some of the best times of their lives,&#8221; said Addy. &#8220;It's such a complicated feeling, and it's always intrigued me.&#8221;

Addy therefore reflected on how while men were out fighting, a huge number of women suddenly found themselves joining the workforce. Relinquishing this from 1945 was anything but easy. &#8220;Those closing months were an uneasy time for women,&#8221; said Addy. &#8220;On the one hand, they're deeply relieved that the war is ending; on the other, they're deeply anxious about their own futures.&#8221;

These thoughts helped crystalize Dig for Victory in Addy's mind. The movie is set in rural Wiltshire, UK, as an isolated girl, Edith, comes of age during the closing months of World War Two. When a violent incident interrupts a new friendship with an older woman, Edith uncovers a series of secrets that will shape a deadly future. blogherads.adq.push(function () { blogherads .defineSlot( 'medrec', 'gpt-dl-mid-article-inject2-uid1' ) .setTargeting( 'pos', ["mid-article2","mid-articleX","mid-article","mid","300x251"] ) .setTargeting( 'viewable', 'yes' ) .setSubAdUnitPath("ros\/mid-article2") .addSize([[300,250],[300,251],[620,350],[2,4],[4,2],[620,366]]) .exemptFromSleep() .setClsOptimization("minsize") ; });

Addy, whose past credits include the BBC and Canal+'s Marie Antoinette and who is working on a project with Scoop producer The Lighthouse, is hoping the Brit list will give the movie a leg up. Dig for Victory received 25 nominations, placing it first alongside Archie Thompson's Petroc. The nominations were unveiled last night at a ceremony in London.

Addy already has ideal cast in mind. While assuming young protagonist Edith would be played by a rising star, Addy's dream for the older woman is the &#8220;charismatic&#8221; and &#8220;captivating&#8221; Indira Varma. For Edith's mother, she settles on Emily Blunt.

Addy thinks the tone she's trying to achieve would be perfect for actors like Varma and Blunt. She was inspired by movies like Florence Pugh-starrer The Wonder and Olivia Colman's Wicked Little Letters, the latter of which is &#8220;darkly comic&#8221; and has a &#8220;main character who felt a little unusual,&#8221; she said. &#8220;I wanted it to be tense and foreboding,&#8221; added Addy. &#8220;Even though the war is at arm's length, there are still planes flying overhead and a sense of threat. I wanted it to feel a little edgy, with twists and turns that keep you on your toes.&#8221;

Addy is hoping she can make something of Dig for Victory but notes it is getting harder for up-and-coming writers to break through, especially since Covid.

&#8220;I heard something quite frightening the other day, which is that they don't really like new writers in rooms at the moment,&#8221; she added. &#8220;They want people who are more established, and that takes a rung off the ladder. But I'm optimistic. I like to think more and more things are getting made.&#8221;

Addy is repped in the UK by The Artists Partnership's Penelope Killick.

